{
  "id": "agentstack-labs/linear",
  "version": "0.2.0",
  "name": "Linear",
  "description": "Batch-triage your Linear backlog behind a human approval gate.\n\nMonday triage is death by a thousand clicks: thirty issues arrived over the weekend and each needs an owner, a priority and a state. Doing it in Linear's UI is thirty round trips. Scripting it against the API is fast but unreviewable — nobody sees what is about to change, and nothing records who approved it. This module stages the whole batch as one command: RailCall renders a preview, a human approves once, and the run leaves a receipt whose approval hash is bound to that exact payload.\n\nWHO IT IS FOR: small engineering teams whose triage, sprint setup and release bookkeeping live in Linear, and who need an audit trail because someone eventually asks who closed those twelve tickets.\n\n36 COMMANDS. Issues (8): list, get, create, update, delete, search, bulk_update, link — relations go through issueRelationCreate, which adds a link without overwriting existing ones. Teams (2), Projects (3), Users (2). Workflow states (3) and Labels (3), for teams that script their board setup. Cycles (4): full sprint lifecycle. Comments (4). Webhooks (4), with explicit team or all-public-teams scoping. Project milestones (3).\n\nCOMPOSES AS A WORKFLOW: list_issues or search_issues to select, bulk_update_issues to act, create_comment to leave an audit note, link_issues to record what blocks the release. If Linear rate-limits mid-batch the command stops and returns not_attempted, so a re-run resumes exactly where it left off.\n\nAIRLOCK GATING: 16 reads execute immediately. All 20 writes are write_requires_approval — each previews, waits for a human, and emits a receipt. Deletes are flagged high risk. Mutations are never auto-retried: Linear's GraphQL API has no idempotency key, so a retry after an accepted write would duplicate it. Recovery is a fresh approval, not a silent replay.\n\nSECURITY: the API key is read from the station vault under the 'linear' provider, never from process environment, never persisted by this module, and never written into a cache key or receipt. Receipts store field names and a hash, not values. All GraphQL arguments travel as typed variables, so input is never interpolated into a query document. Stdlib urllib only — no third-party dependencies.\n\nVerified against a live Linear workspace: all 36 commands exercised end-to-end, plus 199 unit tests. MIT. Source: https://github.com/faizalmy/railcall-linear-module\n\ncontest:2026Q3",
  "author": "AgentStack Labs — RailCall modules for engineering workflow tooling",
  "license": "MIT",
